design:1.architecture:
Root: /Users/man/Documents/GitHub/Tutornat/apps/web/src. All paths below are relative to it. Nothing imports `modules/`; ported text and code is copied in with the THU-MAIC MIT header and listed in `apps/web/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES`.

## 1. The one recorded form: `lib/theater/beat.ts` (new)

Adapted from the `@openmaic/dsl` `action.ts` subset.

```ts
type Cue = { spot: string; word: number }
// word indexes say.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean)

type WidgetCmd =   // discriminated by Widget.kind, values only, never a selector
  | { kind: 'number-line'; at: number }
  | { kind: 'fraction-bar'; parts: number; shaded: number }
  | { kind: 'clock'; h: number; m: number }
  | { kind: 'area-model'; rows: number; cols: number }
  | { kind: 'place-value'; h: number; t: number; o: number }
  | { kind: 'balance'; take: number }
  | { kind: 'states-of-matter'; c: number }
  | { kind: 'moon-phases'; day: number }
  // sorter/sequence/sentence/coordinate get no demo cmd in 1.0

type Beat = {
  say: string;          // display text, markers stripped
  key?: string;         // on-screen segment it reads, so Spoken highlights in place
  cues?: Cue[];         // at most 1 per beat
  show?: WidgetCmd;
  wait?: true;          // hand the turn
}

type Script = {
  v: 1;
  sceneId: string;
  source: 'code' | 'authored' | 'ai';
  beats: Beat[];        // 2-10, say ≤ 220 chars → ≤ 3 KB
  hash: string;
  ai?: { model: string; prompt: 'script.v1'; at: number };
}
```

`lib/types.ts` gets `Scene.script?: Script`. It is optional, so existing data and catalogue files stay valid.

OpenMAIC verbs map into beats as follows:

| OpenMAIC | Beat |
|---|---|
| speech | `say` |
| spotlight / laser before speech | a cue on the word the model names (`look.on`), else word 0 |
| widget_setState | a typed `show` |
| widget_highlight / reveal | a cue on a `widget.*` id |
| discussion | `wait` (the learner's turn, then the live tutor) |
| wb_* | the live tutor's echo-only `show_visual` board card; never in scripts |
| play_video, PBL, multi-agent | dropped |

## 2. Modules

**Ours, new:**
- **`lib/theater/spots.ts`:** `sceneSpots(scene, {answered})`, pure and computed from data, not the DOM. It gives the ids from docs/spotlight.md and spec §6: `stage.title`, `stage.block.<i>`, `stage.block.<i>.visual.<kind>[.part.<k>]`, `widget.*`, `stage.quiz.prompt`. It splits them into pointable and guarded (`stage.choice.*` and `widget.answer` until checked). The cap is 60 ids, the same as `TutorSpotContext`. A component test asserts that the rendered `data-spot` set ⊇ `sceneSpots`.
- **`lib/theater/code-script.ts`:** `codeScript(scene, band, locale)` builds a Script from `slideSegments` / `sceneSegments` (narration.tsx). Every beat is key-bound, so K–2 read-along stays exact. Its cues are the automatic `v{i}` → `stage.block.<i>.visual.<kind>`. A quiz gets one i18n meta line plus `wait`; an interactive gets prompt beats plus `wait`. It is the demo-mode script, the fallback when AI fails, and the default until an AI script exists. It replaces OpenMAIC's hard-coded Chinese fallback.
- **`lib/theater/observe.ts`:** `publicObservation(scene, live)` is built field by field: visible text, the widget's current public state (a number-line value, shaded parts), and the quiz prompt and choices with no index or key. It goes to `TutorContext` as `observation` (≤ 1600 chars) instead of today's `sceneText`.
- **`lib/theater/player.ts`:** a pure state machine (about 200 lines, fake-clock tested). Its states are idle, playing, paused, live, waiting and done.
  - Injected: SpeechOut, the attention scheduler and a widget adapter.
  - It speaks the whole remaining script as one `speak()` iterable of beat texts. Word offsets accumulate, so a cue's global word = the words in earlier beats + `cue.word`.
  - It keeps a generation token, as in OpenMAIC PlaybackEngine.
  - An interrupt saves the cut beat, and resume replays that beat from its first word.
  - Seek re-applies `show` commands silently, and only for controlled widgets; otherwise seek is disabled after the first `show`, following OpenMAIC's action-navigation rule.
- **`lib/theater/timeline.ts`:** adapted from OpenMAIC `choreography/cursor.ts` and `timeline.ts`. It holds a (scene, beat) cursor and builds scrub-bar durations from recorded word times only. Its estimates are never used as word timing.
- **`lib/theater/record.ts`:** session and record logs (see recording). They live in IndexedDB `kaizenedu.theater`, beside `blobs.ts`.
- **`lib/ai/script.ts`:** `writeScript`, `SCRIPT_RULES`, `courseContext` and `gateScript` (see below).
- **`lib/ai/retry.ts`:** a copy of `generation-retry.ts` with maxRetries 2; every attempt is metered.
- **`app/api/ai/script/route.ts`.**
- **UI:**
  - `components/stage/Theater.tsx`: the Play/Pause/Keep-going bar. It replaces NarrationBar's play path; useSpeech's "say it with me" stays.
  - `components/stage/Caption.tsx`: a strip under the scene for beats with no `key`, carrying the current-word highlight.
  - `components/spotlight/cursor.tsx`: the spec §3.4 TutorCursor.
  - `components/theater/Replay.tsx` and `app/(focus)/replay/[id]/page.tsx`.

**Ours, unchanged authority:**
- `Stage.tsx`, `scenes.tsx`, `widgets/*`, `visuals.tsx`
- `lib/spotlight.ts`, plus the spec's pointing session, ring and guards
- `lib/voice/*`, plus the spec's `player.ts`, `cues.ts` and `attention.ts`
- `lib/ai/tutor.ts` (safety screen first, one streamText call)
- `tools.ts`, `safety.ts`, `writeCourse`, `gateWritten`, `gateLesson`
- `learning/*`, `store.ts`, `sync.ts`

**Ported from OpenMAIC (text and patterns):**
- the slide-actions, quiz-actions and interactive-actions rules: one teacher voice, same-session continuity, point before speaking, the quiz answer-safety block, and interactive progression;
- `speech-tts-readability`, adapted: no LaTeX, but normal math notation is allowed because `speakable` reads it, and the dev assertion catches raw symbols;
- `buildCourseContext` (position, greet only on the first scene, close on the last, the last 150 characters of the previous script), in EN and ES;
- `previousSpeeches` threading;
- the Kaizen delta's `TEACHING_DIRECTIVE` (8 rules);
- first-scene-first pipelining;
- stable ids on retry;
- the outline's `keyPoints` (into the per-lesson plan; `objective` already exists);
- the runtime session/record envelope (`dsl` `runtime.ts`).

**Left behind:**
- the PPT canvas and renderer, generated HTML widgets, PBL, discussion and agents;
- the Pi and LangGraph directors and `use-chat-sessions`;
- StreamBuffer, AudioPlayer, the TTS and ASR routes;
- `json-repair`, `action-parser`, the prompt loader;
- `quiz-grade`;
- everything under `lib/ai/*` and `lib/server/model-config` (the OAuth path).

## 3. Script writer (their brain, our types)

```ts
writeScript(
  scene: Scene,
  ctx: { lesson: {title, objective, keyPoints?}; index; total; titles; previous?: string; grade; locale },
  model: LanguageModel,
  signal?
): Promise<Script | null>
```

It runs on `model('build')` with `Output.object` over:

```ts
z.object({ beats: z.array(z.object({
  say: z.string().min(1).max(220),
  look: z.object({ spot: z.enum(pointableIds), on: z.string().max(40) }).optional(),
  show: WidgetCmdSchema(scene.widget).optional(),
  wait: z.boolean().optional(),
})).min(2).max(K2 ? 6 : 10) })
```

The model therefore cannot name a spot that is not on the scene. `look` is omitted from the schema when there are no ids. Code turns `look.on` into a word index by folded match, falling back to 0.

`gateScript` checks, all in code:
- language with `languageOf`;
- K–2 sentences ≤ 10 words, others ≤ 16;
- no praise or hype (`praiseIn`);
- a greeting only on the first scene;
- on quiz scenes, no choice text, answer number or answer word appears in any beat, and there are no cues;
- no cue on a guarded or deictic word (this, here, esto, aquí);
- at most 1 cue per beat and 3 per scene;
- no counting along;
- `show` never equals or reaches the widget target while it is unanswered (`revealsAnswer`);
- interactive and quiz scripts end on `wait`;
- `screen(say)` is ok for every beat.

On failure it retries once with the reasons (the `writeCourse` pattern), then returns null and the `codeScript` stays. The fallback is always in the learner's locale.

## 4. Data flow

**LIVE:**
1. Mic or tap → SpeechIn → `conversation.ts` → `aiFetch` `/api/tutor`.
2. On the server: `screen` → one streamText call with a cached static prompt, ctx `{surface, observation, spots, input}`.
3. The UI stream goes through `cueParser(epoch)`, giving display text plus `Cue[]`.
4. `SpeechOut.speak(sentences)` → `onWordScheduled` → `attention.ts` → the pointing session (ring and cursor). Board tool parts go to Board.
5. The recorder appends `tutor.say`.

**PLAYED:**
1. `Scene.script` (or `codeScript`) → `player.ts` → SpeechOut → the same `onWordScheduled` → `attention.ts`.
2. `show` → the widget adapter.
3. `wait` → the learner's turn. Their check goes through the existing `onAnswer` / `onCheck` and the result is shown to the live tutor or the demo tutor.
4. An interrupt switches to LIVE, then resumes.

**REPLAY:**
1. TheaterRecords → `player.ts` in replay mode.
2. Audio comes from SpeechOut re-synthesis, or captured tutor audio or clips if they exist.
3. Cues go through the same scheduler; widget states come from snapshots.
4. Replay makes zero store writes.

**GENERATE:**
1. A lesson (catalogue, or from `writeCourse`) → `/api/ai/script`, one request per scene, serial, carrying `previous`.
2. The route runs `screen`, `limited(6/min)`, `spendGate('course')` and `meter`. It rebuilds catalogue scenes server-side from ids; generated scenes are client-sent and re-parsed with `SceneSchema` + `gateLesson`.
3. `writeScript` → `gateScript` → the client stores `Scene.script`.
4. Scripts swap only at a scene boundary, never mid-play.

design:2.thesis:
The live tutor is the director, and the theater is its performance kept. Each tutor turn produces one beat, from the one model call per turn the live-tutor spec allows. A beat is the spoken words with [[spot:id]] anchors, plus at most one stage move after the words: put a scene on stage, go to a scene, or start building a lesson.

One Beat shape `{say, cues[{spot, word}], do[], read?, wait?}` is shared by four sources: live AI replies, demo-tutor replies, generated scene scripts and the code-built catalogue scripts. One pure player takes these beats and drives the app SpeechOut, the attention scheduler (glow and cursor) and the stage, all on the voice clock. One append-only record per session stores the beats and the learner's moves. A replay plays that record again. A lesson is either a curated recording or a regenerated one. Lessons multiply because the tutor keeps making them.

From OpenMAIC we take what is solid about its generator:
- its pipeline shape: content, then scene scripts, first scene first, scripts written one after another so each continues the last;
- its scene-script prompts (single voice, same session, point before you say, quiz answer-safety);
- its course context and its retry helper;
- its playback patterns: an interrupt replays the cut sentence, stale work is dropped by epoch, seek rebuilds the stage silently.

That is about 450 copied lines plus about 300 lines of prompt text, with the MIT notice. We leave out:
- its PPTist slide canvas: it is 1000×562 and shows 5–6 px text on a 320 px phone;
- its model-written iframe widgets: no code-checked answers;
- its Pi/LangGraph directors: 2 to 7 model calls per turn;
- all of its model plumbing, where the OAuth adapter lives.

Ground truth checked today:
- **The tutor-branch precondition (spec §0.4) is already met on `foundation`.** Commits 3d3d518, 9dd173f and 7bd494c carry 5cffe4c's work. tools.ts, tutor.ts and cards.ts are byte-identical to 5cffe4c, and Board.tsx and TutorChat.tsx have later fixes. The maps' "blocked on the tutor branch" is stale.
- **`worktree-wf_a6840288-a70-24` must never be merged as a branch.** It predates the content merge: a diff from it to foundation deletes about 127k lines.
- **What is unbuilt and gates this work:** point_at is still a tool, SpotlightLayer is mounted nowhere, no element carries data-spot, and none of lib/voice/{audio,player,cues,conversation}.ts or lib/attention.ts exists yet.

