risks:
Reading 'use the OpenMAIC generator' as the whole package. This design takes its outline, its script stage and its orchestration, and keeps our content stage, for three reasons:
- its PPT canvas scales to about 0.29-0.32 at 320 px, so 18 px text shows at about 5-6 px;
- its quiz keys are model-asserted and only their structure is checked;
- its interactive scenes are model-written HTML/JS, which the one-workspace spec §6-7 excludes.
Escalate with this evidence. The fallback, if the owner insists on literal slides, is porting the roughly 1.2k-line painter subset as a desktop-only tutor board, never the lesson surface.

Spot drift. sceneSpots(scene) is computed in code and must match the ids scenes.tsx renders. A renderer change would silently orphan stored cues. Mitigations:
- a component test over all 1,267 catalogue scenes;
- fire-time resolveSpot drops missing targets silently;
- a script stores its sceneSpots version and is re-gated on mismatch.

Ported prompt text carries behaviour we ban:
- 'Encouragements … (e.g., "Great job, everyone!")' (slide-actions/system.md), which praiseIn() must catch;
- discussion and multi-agent rules;
- Chinese examples;
- adult pedagogy ('a quiz every 3-5 slides').
Port the rules as edited TS constants under golden tests, never the templates verbatim.

Answer leaks through the theater:
- a quiz beat previewing or comparing choices;
- a pose set to the target;
- an anchor on a guarded value part;
- counting along a number line.
Gates at write time (gateScript, poseProblems), guards at fire time, and the no-counting-along limit in attention.ts all apply. A test runs every catalogue item's possible targets against the guards.

Honesty regressions:
- a replay counted as practice, help or proof;
- a demo pose marking checks assisted, or failing to mark the ones it should;
- 'tutor present' recorded as 'tutor helped'.
The replay engine has no import path to the recorder, acts or activity (a lint boundary plus a store-diff test). Whether a pose differing from the target counts as teaching, like a slide, rather than help needs the owner's or a reviewer's sign-off before P3 ships.

Cost. Retries stack:
- OpenMAIC defaults to 5 client retries over server retries;
- 'another way' variants add calls;
- original-voice re-renders of live takes are paid.
Cap retries at 2 per stage, meter every attempt through budget.ts, limit make_lesson to 3 per learner per day, cache variants and clips, and default to silent replay.

scores.notes:
Closest to the owner's literal words. The tutor is the director, and lessons are its performance kept. stage_scene and go_to_scene are echo-only tools inside the one tutor call, so in Talk the tutor can put a fraction bar in front of the child mid-conversation: show, let them touch it, the widget's own check(), then it points at the result next turn (observe.ts sends the stage state with no target). Every session is recorded at T2, straight after the tutor. curateSession turns a recording into a gated lesson in code, with no model call. 'Make another like this' and thumbs up/down multiply lessons. It is the smallest build (about 1,000 new lines) and reuses the existing Board and cards. Its ground truth checks out: git cherry gives 17 identical and 2 reworked patches; there is no data-spot outside the spotlight module; point_at is still in spot-tool.ts; course/route.ts has no screen(). Its warning about worktree-wf_a6840288-a70-24 is real (merging it would delete 147,130 lines). Weaknesses:
- cues in generated scripts carry a numeric word index the model must count;
- stage_scene asks the model to write widget configs live, so a fact-level error is only labelled and gated, never checked;
- make_lesson has no tap and no daily cap;
- it rejects keeping live audio on PCM size (58 MB per 20 minutes);
- it skips OpenMAIC's outline stage for single lessons, so it is a little less 'their generator';
- the day estimates for T3 (controlled state on 12 widgets) and T4 are optimistic.
